<article class="env-lemma" id="0202">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0202">Lemma <span data-tag="0202">32.12.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a quasi-compact and quasi-separated scheme. Let $f : X \to S$ be a separated morphism of finite type. Then there exists an $n \geq 0$ and a diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X \ar[rd] &  X' \ar[d] \ar[l]^\pi \ar[r] &  \mathbf{P}^ n_ S \ar[dl] \\  &  S &  }  \]
</div>
  <p> where $X' \to \mathbf{P}^ n_ S$ is an immersion, and $\pi : X' \to X$ is proper and surjective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Proposition <a href="/tag/01ZJ" data-tag="01ZJ">32.9.6</a> we can find a closed immersion $X \to Y$ where $Y$ is separated and of finite presentation over $S$. Clearly, if we prove the assertion for $Y$, then the result follows for $X$. Hence we may assume that $X$ is of finite presentation over $S$. 
    <p>
      Write $S = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ i S_ i$ as a directed limit of Noetherian schemes, see Proposition <a href="/tag/01ZA" data-tag="01ZA">32.5.4</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/01ZM" data-tag="01ZM">32.10.1</a> we can find an index $i \in I$ and a scheme $X_ i \to S_ i$ of finite presentation so that $X = S \times _{S_ i} X_ i$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/01ZQ" data-tag="01ZQ">32.8.6</a> we may assume that $X_ i \to S_ i$ is separated. Clearly, if we prove the assertion for $X_ i$ over $S_ i$, then the assertion holds for $X$. The case $X_ i \to S_ i$ is treated by Cohomology of Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0200" data-tag="0200">30.18.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>